  "articlePanels": [
    {
      "kind": "lede",
      "html": "<p>A manager picks an inspection app with care. She compares templates, weighs the price, sits through the demo and runs the trial. Six weeks later her team has quietly gone back to paper.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "takeaways",
      "heading": "The argument, briefly",
      "html": "<ul><li>Field software fails on use far more often than on features. The tool works. The team does not open it.</li><li>The real barrier is the small act of opening a separate app.</li><li>A cheaper app, a stronger app or more training does not fix that.</li><li>Quickler removes the app. You answer in WhatsApp.</li><li>The trade-off: Quickler is not a drag-and-drop form builder with thousands of templates.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "The wrong cause",
      "heading": "It is not the features, and not the price",
      "html": "<p>When an app goes unused, firms blame features, cost or training. So they buy another app, and six weeks later they are in the same place.</p><p>The buyer compares features at a desk, calm and unhurried. The worker decides on site, in a hurry, with a job in hand. The buying process only hears from the first.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "The real barrier",
      "heading": "The cost of opening a separate thing",
      "html": "<p>To use an app, a worker must stop, find it, open it, wait, remember the login and find the right form. Each step is small. Together, with hands full, they are enough to lose to.</p><p>So the check waits until later. Later means the desk, and the desk means memory. The record fades.</p><p>In a demo, everyone is seated and calm, so you never see it.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Why more of the same fails",
      "heading": "A better app does not fix it",
      "html": "<p>A cheaper app is still an app to open. A stronger app has more screens to open. More training teaches people to use something they choose not to open.</p><p>The only fix that reaches the cause is to remove the separate app. Run the check where the worker already is.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "The medium is the decision",
      "heading": "Meet people where they already are",
      "html": "<p>Almost everyone on a field job already has WhatsApp open all day. It is installed, logged in and a reflex.</p><p>Quickler does not build a better inspection app. It removes the app. A question arrives in the chat you already glance at, and answering it is like replying to a colleague. A line of text, a voice note or a photo.</p><p>The trade-off is honest. A dedicated form builder offers more set-up options. If your team will happily open an app, that may suit you. But if use is where your last tool died, the answer is not another app. It is no new app.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "faq",
      "heading": "Questions, answered",
      "html": "<h3>Our last inspection app was good. Why did the team stop using it?</h3><p>Almost always because of the small hassle of opening a separate app and logging in at the point of work. That hassle is invisible in a demo and decisive on site.</p><h3>Would more training fix it?</h3><p>Rarely. Training teaches people to use a tool they choose not to open. Removing the separate app fixes the cause.</p><h3>How is WhatsApp different?</h3><p>It is already installed and logged in, and most people use it daily. Answering is like replying to a colleague. No new app to find, no password, no loading screen.</p><h3>What is the trade-off?</h3><p>Quickler is not a drag-and-drop form builder with thousands of templates. If your team will reliably open an app, a form builder may suit you. Quickler also is not a care records or clinical system. It sits alongside one.</p><h3>Related reading</h3><ul><li><a href=\"/pages/the-report-written-from-memory.html\">The report written from memory</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/paperless-inspections-uk-trades.html\">Paperless inspections</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/inspection-app-uk-no-training.html\">The inspection app your team will actually use</a></li></ul>"
    }
